WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our first guest tonight
is a very funny actor, writer,

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.404
and director who you know from
"The Mindy Project"

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:06.372
and movies like "Suicide Squad"
and "Blockers."

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.441
He executive-produces and stars

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.843
in the animated series
"Chicago Party Aunt,"

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:12.178
which is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.514
Let's take a look.
-Look at those paws.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:18.051
What are you? A carpenter?
-No, carpentry is just a hobby.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:20.052
I'm actually a firefighter.

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:22.455
-Oh! Oh, gosh.

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:25.090
Back...room...now.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:26.459
[ Buzzer ]

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:29.529
-Diane, you just broke every
single rule in 12 seconds.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.964
-Gideon, not now!

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:34.167
-This man is a hired actor.
You knew this!

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.103
Diane, he has been role-playing
all morning!

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.771
-You're an actor?

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:41.440
-I also played a coma victim
on "Chicago Med."

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.776
-Oh! A double gush!

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:47.080
Okay, this is entrapment.
I got witnesses.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.481
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:50.516
our very good friend
Ike Barinholtz.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.985
How are you, buddy?
-My man.

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:55.821
-Look at that. Representing,
Thank you so much.

00:00:55.821 --> 00:00:59.492
-I'm representing,
but I got a purpose here.

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.093
So, I've had this shirt
for a while.

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:05.197
A few weeks ago, made a little
chicken piccata for the family.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:08.301
And I got...
-You stained it.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.203
-...stains here, here, and here.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.571
-Okay, so, you want
another one?

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.806
And this time we'll try to
get it for you in your size.

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:17.443
-That's what I'm getting at.
Hey, listen -- Okay, okay.

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:19.979
I've put on a couple pounds
over quarantine.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.749
Who hasn't?
-You basically admitted

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.984
you're out there making piccata.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.519
-Well, I mean, I have to feed

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.288
my people, Seth.
My God.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:31.157
Listen, X-L,
whatever color you guys want.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.058
I see the dark blue.
They look real nice.

00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:36.228
Adam Scott wore one.
I would love one of those.

00:01:36.228 --> 00:01:38.665
At your leisure. No rush.
-Alright, thank you, buddy.

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:41.333
I am always genuinely so happy
to see you.

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:42.502
I wish you were here in person.

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.804
But I'm so very happy
about this project.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:47.573
You are, of course,
a son of Chicago.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.909
-Yes!
-And this is a loving --

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.313
a loving portrait of
Chicago people, this cartoon.

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:55.715
-Chicago has given me
so many gifts,

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:57.483
and I thought it was time
to give something back

00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:00.954
to the city of broad shoulders.
-Yeah.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:06.559
-And so, we -- you know,
our friend Chris Witaske,

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.528
who you know,
started this Twitter feed

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.963
many years ago
called "Chicago Party Aunt,"

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:13.799
and it was funny and
kind of goofy and stuff.

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:15.467
And we would interact with it.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:18.504
And then, one day, our friend
Richie Schwartz said,

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.539
"Hey, I think this could be
a funny animated show."

00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:22.675
And Netflix bought it,

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.578
and now this insanely stupid
inside joke

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:29.248
between me and a few friends
is in like 400 countries.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.384
[ Laughter ]
It's crazy.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:33.719
-Would you admit that --
So, you were --

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:36.623
You were born in the '80s
in Chicago.

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:40.126
-The late '70s, but thank you.
-But so you were old enough

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.994
to appreciate the '86 bears,

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:43.630
and then you lived through
the Jordan Bulls.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.364
And I have always felt,
even though I have

00:02:45.364 --> 00:02:47.667
a great many friends
who that is true of,

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.969
that you are some of the most
insufferable people on Earth.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:52.504
-Well, I mean, that comes --

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:55.608
That's a lot, coming from
a Red Sox and a Steelers fan.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.176
-Yeah, that's true.

00:02:57.176 --> 00:02:59.845
-But, yeah -- no, Chicago fans,
we are very entitled.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:02.448
We still consider the Bears
an elite football team,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.650
even though the last time
we won a Super Bowl,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.585
"Miami Vice" was
the number-one show on TV.

00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:08.955
[ Laughter ]
But, you know, we're babies.

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.656
We're definitely babies
with our sports.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:13.559
But we're sweet babies.

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:16.362
-How has Netflix been?
You've worked with

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.864
a great many studios
over your illustrious career.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.266
This is your first time
with Netflix.

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.735
How have they been so far?
-I'm in the algorithm.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:26.806
I'm in there now.
I'm running stuff, you know --

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.941
-They've given you access
to the algorithm?

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:33.446
-I now am the one who kind of --
behind the scenes,

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:36.749
me and Ted Sarandos
are kind of moving stuff around.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.351
It's fun. It's really cool.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:42.322
It's so cool that it's
in so many different countries.

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:44.556
The good thing
about "Chicago Party Aunt,"

00:03:44.556 --> 00:03:47.192
it's probably the same name
in every other country.

00:03:47.192 --> 00:03:48.861
You know, like, other countries

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:53.165
the show is called, like,
"Aunt Lois in Latvia."

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:54.500
We called it
"Chicago Party Aunt,"

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.768
so we just cut out
the middle man,

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.537
so it's great that
all these different countries

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:01.640
are gonna see the insanely silly
jokes we put up.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.411
-You, of course, had
your fair share of parties

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.847
in Chicago over the years.
And you had a pretty --

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:11.884
For a young man who wanted
to be involved in comedy,

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:15.755
you got some pretty-early
interactions with some legends.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:18.825
-Yeah, well, you know,
we both went through

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:21.994
the Chicago improv scene
in the mid- to late '90s.

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:25.097
And it was super exciting
just because you would,

00:04:25.097 --> 00:04:28.834
you know, get to watch, like,
Tina and Rachel Dratch

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:32.305
and Kevin Dorff onstage.
But you would also get, like,

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.407
the old legends
that would come back.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:37.910
And I got to meet Chris Farley
one time -- super cool.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.845
Like, I walked into
the e.t.c. Theater --

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:41.714
like, the other theater --
and it was dark.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:44.684
And he was in there by himself,
maybe smoking a joint.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:48.387
I don't know. I can't remember.
But I got to hang out with him.

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.389
And I remember thinking like,
"I'm -- This is it.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:52.358
This is the pinnacle
of my career.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.860
I got to meet Chris Farley."

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.996
And like four months later,
we were at the Last Act,

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.365
the bar across the street,
and he walked in and saw me

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:01.500
and goes, "Do you know
where my brother is?!"

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.236
And I was like, "Oh, yeah,
he's at this party."

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:05.605
And I said to my friend
from high school,

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:07.773
"Do you mind if we drive
Chris Farley to this party?"

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.875
And we were driving
and he goes,

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:11.343
"I got to pee, I got to pee!"

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:13.979
And we pulled out, like,
underneath the "L" tracks,

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:15.514
and I was like,
"I got pee, too."

00:05:15.514 --> 00:05:17.883
And as we're peeing,
Chris is like,

00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:20.253
"Do you want to play Swords?"

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.455
You know,
the classic game Swords.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:24.557
You're with your young friends
and you're both peeing,

00:05:24.557 --> 00:05:27.593
and you kind of...
cross the streams.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.128
-Yeah, it's a fun game.
-I'm like, "Yeah, sure!"

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:31.130
It's a very fun game.
I hadn't played it

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.566
since I was 7 or 8,
but it's Chris Farley.

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.701
So we kind of played Swords
for a second.

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.803
Then, he just turns
and just pees all up my leg

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:40.340
and just started, like,
cackling hilariously.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.110
And, uh, yeah,
still have the jeans.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.111
Haven't washed them.
Still wear them.

00:05:46.111 --> 00:05:47.946
[ Laughter ]
They stink.

00:05:47.946 --> 00:05:50.583
But it's a nice memory.
-It is a nice memory.

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:52.351
I have a lot more
to ask you about.

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:54.420
We'll be right back
with more from Ike Barinholtz.

